# FocalTech fingerprint sensor node and the QSEECom path to its trusted app,
# bound to the fingerprint HAL (stock ff_device label). Each grant is the set
# the stock module used on permissive boots before these rules existed: open,
# read, write and ioctl on the node and on QSEECom; open, read and ioctl on
# the read-only (0444) QSEECom heaps. Templates live in
# /data/vendor_de/<user>/fpdata (fingerprint_vendor_data_file, created by
# vold), which the platform hal_fingerprint rules already cover.
type ff_device, dev_type;
allow hal_fingerprint_default ff_device:chr_file { open read write ioctl };
allow hal_fingerprint_default tee_device:chr_file { open read write ioctl };
allow hal_fingerprint_default { vendor_dmabuf_qseecom_heap_device vendor_dmabuf_qseecom_ta_heap_device }:chr_file { open read ioctl };

# The module also registers a factory/debug binder service,
# FocalFingerprintService, whose one dispatcher (invokeCommand) has no caller
# check. It is deliberately left unlabelled: it falls back to
# default_android_service, on which the platform forbids every add, find and
# list rule, so no policy can ever grant it. The module's only switch for it
# is a /data config file, which is deliberately not created.

# That /data config (/data/vendor/fingerprint/ft9391/Private/ff_config.json)
# can retune matching and lockout, and the module's dump homes are under
# /data/vendor too. Nothing may ever let the HAL open files there.
neverallow hal_fingerprint_default vendor_data_file:file { open read };
